Subject: [PATCH] build: a js/wasm profile for the Ptah CLI, and one shared runner

Two changes, both of which stand on their own upstream.

1. A js/wasm build profile. Two dependencies cannot link on js/wasm:
   github.com/sijms/go-ora/v3 reaches syscall.Sendmsg and MSG_OOB, and
   modernc.org/sqlite needs mmap and OS file locking. Neither can be
   reached from a browser anyway -- there is no socket to an Oracle
   listener and no page-aligned file to map -- so each import moves
   behind a !js file and gets a js counterpart that either states the
   absence (Oracle) or defers to whatever the host installed
   (ptah.run/internal/browsersqlite).

   internal/devlock, internal/atlasmigrate and internal/testutils gain js
   flock and stat shims for the same reason: their _unix.go/_windows.go
   pair leaves js with no implementation at all. The two production locks
   succeed, because a single wasm instance is the only holder of its own
   filesystem. The test helper refuses, because it exists to prove
   cross-process behavior and js/wasm has no second process.

   After this, GOOS=js GOARCH=wasm go build ./... is clean for the whole
   module.

   The js side of the SQLite seam, ptah.run/internal/browsersqlite, is
   not in this patch. It is the host's to supply.

2. cmd/root grows an exported RunContext(ctx, cmd, args...) int, and the
   unexported runCommand becomes the native wrapper around it. The split
   is along the line of what only a process has: signal delivery and
   os.Exit stay in runCommand and ExecuteCommand, while the run itself --
   set args, set context, execute with panic recovery, map the error
   through exitcode.Code -- is callable by anything holding a context.
   A caller that is not a process (a js/wasm host, a test) gets the exit
   status without the exit, and cancels through its own context rather
   than through a signal watcher that can never fire.

   Native behavior is unchanged: runCommand still installs the interrupt
   watcher first, still lets an interrupt override the command's own
   status through interruptExitCode, and still tears the watcher down
   before ExecuteCommand calls os.Exit.

Not covered: internal/dbschema/oracle/roles_internal_test.go still
imports github.com/sijms/go-ora/v3/network directly, so that package's
tests do not compile for js. Tests are not built for js in any gate, and
the test is asserting on a driver error type that only exists where the
driver links.

